  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nCold front moving east across far eastern NY into western New \nEngland through the rest of the afternoon. A low-topped line of \nconvection developed along and just east of the front. Gusty \nwinds of mainly 40-50 mph are expected, although a few damaging \nwind gusts cannot be ruled out due to strong wind shear. There \nis a low probability (2-4%) of a brief/isolated tornado mainly \nin the N. Berkshires into S. Vermont where effective helicity of\nhighest. Otherwise, widely scattered flash flooding may occur \nwhere there is any training especially across areas that \nreceived heavy rainfall early this morning such as in \nDutchess/Litchfield Counties. The threat for any severe/flash \nflooding will quickly end by later this afternoon around 4 pm as\nthe cold front moves east out of our area. \n\nIt will turn breezy and less humid behind the cold front, with \nwesterly winds gusting 20-30 mph into the evening. Winds will \nsubside later tonight, with patchy fog developing late.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMore tranquil weather is expected for Tue and also Wed, with \nhigh pressure in place. Humidity levels will be more comfortable\ncompared to today. Model guidance is indicating a very \nsmall/compact low moving onshore near SE New England, but latest\ntrends are for this system to remain south/east of our area. \n\nBy Thu, moisture and humidity will increase again as a \npersistent south-southwest flow develops around the periphery of\na Bermuda high. Dewpoints are expected to rise into the 70s, \nwith PWAT anomalies increasing to +1 to +2 STDEV. There does \nappear to be a lack of synoptic scale forcing through Thu \nmorning, as a frontal system is expected to be positioned \nnorth/west of Lake Ontario with no significant push to the south\ndue to the strengthening Bermuda high. Eventually the front \nshould slowly drift south/east into our area later Thu into Fri,\nwhich is when chances for showers/T-storms increase. Due to \nuncertainty in coverage/timing, there is low confidence in \nsevere storm and/or flash flood potential. AI NWP convective \nhazards forecast shows at least some potential for severe storms\nduring this time. Also, with such a humid/moist environment and\nwet antecedent ground conditions, there will likely be a threat\nof at least isolated flash flooding.",
